Arootinstall
============

Arootinstall is the AOS live-system installer. It is exposed through the native
MUI Install AOS application and through the `arootinstall` command. Both front
ends use the same kernel installer transaction and report the same progress.

Availability
------------

- Installation is available from live media only.
- The live ISO must contain both installer boot payload modules.
- The selected target must be a writable hardware block device of at least
  32 MiB.
- Installed systems reject a second installer run.

MUI
---

Open Activities, then select Install AOS. The wizard covers the eleven sections
defined in `Installations_Options`: account, partitioning, language, region,
timezone, Wi-Fi profile, driver scan, applications, keyboard, hostname, and
final review.

Text mode
---------

Run the interactive installer:

    arootinstall

Useful non-interactive commands:

    arootinstall --status
    arootinstall --dry-run
    arootinstall --install-defaults --target 0 --password 'StrongPass1' --confirm AOS
    arootinstall --install-defaults --target 0 --password 'StrongPass1' \
        --username explorer --standard --autologin --confirm AOS

`Arootinstall` and `install-aos` are aliases. Unattended installation requires
`--install-defaults`, a password of at least eight characters, and the exact
`--confirm AOS` argument. `--standard` creates a non-administrator account;
the default is administrator. `--autologin` skips the password screen after
boot. Replace the example password before using these commands.

Disk layout and boot
--------------------

Automatic mode erases the selected disk and creates:

- a 16 MiB FAT boot partition beginning at 1 MiB;
- an AOSFS system partition beginning at 17 MiB and using the remaining space;
- GRUB boot support for legacy BIOS and x86_64 UEFI.

Manual mode opens or directs the user to the `partition` command. The installer
does not erase a disk from manual mode until a FAT boot plus AOSFS root layout
can be validated; unsupported manual layouts are rejected instead of guessed.

Safety and completion
---------------------

The final review names the target disk and requires the user to type `AOS`.
Cancellation is accepted only before disk modification starts. The transaction
writes the boot payload, formats AOSFS, writes account and system settings,
installs GRUB, creates `logs/install.log`, and verifies the MBR, FAT boot sector,
AOSFS superblock, and completion marker. The completion marker is written last.

Remove the live media before rebooting into the installed system.

First boot and accounts
-----------------------

Installed systems start at the native MUI sign-in screen unless autologin was
selected. Passwords are stored as salted PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 records with 12,000
iterations; plaintext passwords are never written to disk. Existing legacy
`$aos$` records remain readable for migration, but new installations never
create them.

Use the top-bar power menu to sign out, or press Ctrl+Alt+L. Signing out stops
the account's child processes and returns the desktop manager to the restricted
login identity.

Administrator accounts may use `sudo COMMAND` and must enter their password.
Standard accounts cannot use sudo. Installed user processes can write under
`/main`, `/tmp`, and their trash area; protected system paths require sudo.

See `docs/session-manager.md` for the kernel/MUI session contract.
